  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over the next two years, the ILO CO-Brasilia aims to make significant strides in collaboration with Brazilian constituents, targeting the advancement of labor rights and the promotion of decent work nationwide. By the end of 2025, Brazil is anticipated to have ratified key international conventions, including C190, C156, and Protocol 29, signaling a commitment to aligning labor laws with global standards and prioritizing worker well-being. The Ministry of Labor and Employment (MTE) is projected to complete comprehensive reporting on the application of conventions C100, C169, and C111 by the end of 2025. This reporting process will offer crucial insights into convention implementation, facilitating progress monitoring and identifying areas for labor practice improvement. Additionally, efforts are underway to support the development and approval of the Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP), ensuring alignment with Brazil&apos;s priorities and international labor standards. In tandem with efforts to mainstream labor standards into broader development agendas, the United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework (UNSDCF) is expected to incorporate new activities related to international labor standards by 2025. This integration underscores the significance of incorporating labor rights into sustainable development initiatives. Moreover, technical cooperation initiatives aim to enhance transparency and accountability in convention implementation, while voluntary conciliation efforts seek to foster constructive labor relations and resolve disputes peacefully, fostering a more harmonious work environment in Brazil.</narrative>
  </description>
